## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical amortized analysis under streaming appends with cache considerations. It evaluates understanding of how to design data structures that balance update frequency, query latency, and memory access patterns.

## Key Concepts
- **Sliding windows**: Maintaining a fixed-size view over streaming data
- **Circular buffers**: O(1) rotation without shifting elements
- **Amortized analysis**: Batched updates spread costs over time
- **Cache-friendly layout**: Sequential memory access patterns for spatial locality

## Code Example
```javascript
class StreamingAnalyticsStore {
  constructor(W, kQueries, mKeys) {
    this.W = W;
    this.blocks = [];
    this.head = 0;
    this.size = 0;
    this.windowSum = 0;
    this.queryRanges = kQueries.map(([start, end]) => ({ start, end }));
    this.keySet = new Set(mKeys);
  }

  appendBatch(newBlocks) {
    for (const block of newBlocks) {
      if (this.size === this.W) {
        this.windowSum -= this.blocks[this.head];
      }
      this.blocks[this.head] = block;
      this.windowSum += block;
      this.head = (this.head + 1) % this.W;
      this.size = Math.min(this.size + 1, this.W);
    }
  }

  rangeSum(queryIndex) {
    const { start, end } = this.queryRanges[queryIndex];
    const windowSize = this.size;
    const startIdx = (this.head - end + this.W) % this.W;
    const endIdx = (this.head - start - 1 + this.W) % this.W;
    let sum = 0;
    let i = startIdx;
    while (i !== (endIdx + 1) % this.W) {
      sum += this.blocks[i];
      i = (i + 1) % this.W;
    }
    return sum;
  }

  hasKey(key) {
    return this.keySet.has(key);
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question explores advanced incremental data-structure design under streaming constraints, requiring mastery of amortized analysis for batched updates alongside exact substring query capabilities.

## Key Concepts

- **Suffix Automaton fundamentals**: States, transitions, links, and the endpos equivalence relation
- **Batched extension strategies**: Buffering B characters before committing to the global structure
- **Clone management and amortized cost**: Deferred clone creation and lazy propagation techniques
- **Transition representations**: Compact maps vs. dense arrays for cache locality
- **Trade-off analysis**: Balancing batch size B, alphabet size σ, and total string length N

## Code Example

```cpp
struct State {
    int link;
    int len;
    std::unordered_map<char, int> next;  // or vector<int> for small σ
};

class BatchedSuffixAutomaton {
    std::vector<State> st;
    int last;
    std::string buffer;
    const size_t B;

public:
    BatchedSuffixAutomaton(size_t batch_size = 1024) : B(batch_size), last(0) {
        st.push_back({-1, 0, {}});
    }

    void extend(char c) {
        buffer.push_back(c);
        if (buffer.size() < B) return;
        flushBuffer();
    }

    void flushBuffer() {
        for (char c : buffer) {
            int cur = st.size();
            st.push_back({0, st[last].len + 1, {}});
            int p = last;
            while (p >= 0 && !st[p].next.count(c)) {
                st[p].next[c] = cur;
                p = st[p].link;
            }
            if (p == -1) {
                st[cur].link = 0;
            } else {
                int q = st[p].next[c];
                if (st[p].len + 1 == st[q].len) {
                    st[cur].link = q;
                } else {
                    int clone = st.size();
                    st.push_back(st[q]);  // clone
                    st[clone].len = st[p].len + 1;
                    while (p >= 0 && st[p].next[c] == q) {
                        st[p].next[c] = clone;
                        p = st[p].link;
                    }
                    st[q].link = st[cur].link = clone;
                }
            }
            last = cur;
        }
        buffer.clear();
    }

    bool query(const std::string& p) {
        int v = 0;
        for (char c : p) {
            if (!st[v].next.count(c)) return false;
            v = st[v].next[c];
        }
        return true;
    }
};
```

## Optimal Batch Size

The optimal B depends on workload characteristics:
- **Small σ (e.g., DNA, σ ≤ 4)**: Larger B (4096+) works well; dense transition arrays gain from cache locality
- **Large σ (e.g., Unicode, σ ≈ 10⁵)**: Smaller B (128–512); sparse maps reduce memory pressure
- **Memory-constrained environments**: B ≈ √N balances update frequency against per-batch overhead
- **Latency-sensitive queries**: Smaller B reduces worst-case query delay between flushes

