## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical knowledge of streaming quantiles and the trade-offs between memory, accuracy, and latency. It also evaluates familiarity with mergeable sketches for distributed systems.

## Key Concepts
- Streaming quantiles (TDigest/KLL)
- Mergeable data sketches across shards
- Compression parameter and accuracy vs memory trade-offs
- Update and query time complexities

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode: TDigest usage
class TDigest { 
  add(x); 
  quantile(p); 
  merge(other); 
}

const digest = new TDigest(100);
for (const latency of batch) {
  digest.add(latency);
}
const p95 = digest.quantile(0.95);
```

## Performance Comparison
- **TDigest**: O(n log k) total time, O(k) memory, O(log k) per-element update
- **Recomputing**: O(n log n) total time, O(n) memory, O(1) per-element (but requires storage)

The streaming approach enables real-time 95th percentile computation with strict memory limits and supports distributed merging across shards.

## Why This Is Asked
Dynamic SSSP with weight-decreases mirrors real-time route/latency updates. This tests practical algorithm design, data-structure choices, and amortized reasoning for production graphs.

## Key Concepts
- Dynamic shortest paths under monotone updates
- Dijkstra-like propagation with lazy updates
- Priority queues vs bucketed approaches for integer weights
- Amortized analysis based on updated edges and affected nodes
- Memory footprint: O(N+M)

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode for batch update
function updateBatch(changes) {
  for (const {u,v,w} of changes) {
    if (dist[u] + w < dist[v]) {
      dist[v] = dist[u] + w;
      pq.push(v);
    }
  }
  while (!pq.isEmpty()) {
    const x = pq.pop();
    for (const e of adj[x]) {
      if (dist[x] + e.w < dist[e.v]) {
        dist[e.v] = dist[x] + e.w; pq.push(e.v);
      }
    }
  }
}
```

